### C Program to Store Student Information using Structures

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

struct Student {
    int roll_no;
    char name[50];
    float gpa;
};

void displayStudent(const struct Student *s) {
    printf("Roll No: %d, Name: %s, GPA: %.2f\n", s->roll_no, s->name, s->gpa);
}

int main() {
    int n;
    printf("Enter number of students: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    // Dynamically allocating structures using pointers
    struct Student *students = (struct Student *)malloc(n * sizeof(struct Student));

    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("\nEnter details for student %d:\n", i+1);
        printf("Roll No: ");
        scanf("%d", &(students + i)->roll_no);
        printf("Name: ");
        scanf("%s", (students + i)->name);
        printf("GPA: ");
        scanf("%f", &(students + i)->gpa);
    }

    printf("\n--- Student Records ---\n");
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // Passing pointer to structure for improved efficiency
        displayStudent(&students[i]);
    }

    free(students);
    return 0;
}

```

### How Pointers Improve Efficiency
1. **Pass-by-Reference vs Pass-by-Value:** Passing a large structure directly copy-by-value replicates the entire structure in the call stack, consuming extra space and CPU cycles. Passing a pointer to the structure (typically only 4 or 8 bytes) is much faster.
2. **Direct Memory Modification:** Pointers allow functions to update structure values directly without returning a copy of the entire structure.
3. **Efficient Traversals:** Array element navigation using pointer arithmetic (`*(ptr + i)`) is highly efficient at the machine-code level.

### Deletion from a Doubly Linked List
In a Doubly Linked List (DLL), each node contains pointers to both its successor (`next`) and predecessor (`prev`). This simplifies deletion since we can access the preceding node directly without a sequential traversal.

### Deletion Logic Scenarios

#### 1. Deleting the Head Node:

```text
[Head]                        
   [Node 1] <===> [Node 2] <===> [Node 3]
      |             ^
     (Free)         |   (New Head's Prev becomes NULL)

```

#### 2. Deleting a Middle Node `curr`:

```text
[Node A] <===> [curr] <===> [Node B]
      \___________________________/
                  Link Skip

```

### C Routine for Deletion of a Node

```c
void deleteNode(struct Node** head_ref, struct Node* del_node) {
    if (*head_ref == NULL || del_node == NULL) 
        return;

    // If node to be deleted is head node
    if (*head_ref == del_node) {
        *head_ref = del_node->next;
    }

    // Change next only if node to be deleted is NOT the last node
    if (del_node->next != NULL) {
        del_node->next->prev = del_node->prev;
    }

    // Change prev only if node to be deleted is NOT the first node
    if (del_node->prev != NULL) {
        del_node->prev->next = del_node->next;
    }

    free(del_node);
}
```
